---
import Marketing from '@layouts/Marketing.astro';

// Raw markup, lifted verbatim from the page this replaces.
const body = `<div class="shell">
  <section class="band doc" style="border-top:none">
    <p class="eyebrow">Pricing</p>
    <h2>One price. Paid once.</h2>
    <p class="sub">No subscription, no account, no in-app purchases, no tiers to compare.
    You buy the app, you own the app.</p>

    <div class="pricecard">
      <div class="priceline">
        <span class="amount">$6.99</span>
        <span class="once">once</span>
      </div>
      <p class="pricenote">Launch price · iPhone and iPad · US pricing, converted automatically elsewhere</p>
      <p class="pricecta">Coming to the App Store</p>
      <p class="pricefine">This is the launch price, and it goes up as the app matures —
      never down. Buying early is the discount; there will not be a sale later.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Everything is included</h3>
    <p class="txt">There is no Pro tier and nothing is held back. The list below is not a
    plan — it is the app.</p>
    <ul class="list">
      <li><b>SSH and Mosh</b> — Mosh sessions survive Wi-Fi/5G handoffs and heavy packet loss.</li>
      <li><b>Native tmux and herdr</b> — real sessions, windows and panes, not a TUI in a box.</li>
      <li><b>Agent status on the lock screen</b> — Allow, Deny and quick replies without unlocking.</li>
      <li><b>Isolated tasks on a git worktree</b> — start one from the phone, in one sheet.</li>
      <li><b>Keyboard, themes, icon and accent</b> — a shortcut bar you build from a key library.</li>
      <li><b>No account and no servers of ours</b> — nothing to sign up for, nothing collected.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What it costs against a subscription</h3>
    <p class="txt">The comparison that matters for a tool you will still be using in three
    years. Subscription prices checked August 2026; a terminal you pay for once has no
    second column.</p>

    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>After…</th><th class="us">Moshpit</th><th>Termius Pro</th>
              <th>Moshi</th><th>Blink+</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="cap-col">Year 1</td><td class="us"><span class="yes">$6.99</span></td>
              <td class="no">$120</td><td class="no">$69.99</td><td class="no">$19.99</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="cap-col">Year 2</td><td class="us"><span class="yes">$6.99</span></td>
              <td class="no">$240</td><td class="no">$139.98</td><td class="no">$39.98</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="cap-col">Year 3</td><td class="us"><span class="yes">$6.99</span></td>
              <td class="no">$360</td><td class="no">$209.97</td><td class="no">$59.97</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="cap-col">Year 5</td><td class="us"><span class="yes">$6.99</span></td>
              <td class="no">$600</td><td class="no">$349.95</td><td class="no">$99.95</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="txt">One month of Termius Pro costs more than Moshpit does, forever. That is
    the whole pitch, and it is the reason the price is allowed to rise later without the
    argument changing.</p>

    <h3>The price goes up</h3>
    <p class="txt">Saying so in public is the point. A launch price that quietly becomes
    permanent is just a price; one with the ladder published is a reason to decide now.
    Nobody who has already bought is ever charged again.</p>

    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th class="us">Price</th><th>When it moves</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="cap-col">Launch</td><td class="us"><span class="yes">$6.99</span></td>
              <td class="no">Now, at v1.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="cap-col">Standard</td><td class="us">$9.99</td>
              <td class="no">Announced in advance, once the app has paid for its own developer account</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>The details people actually ask about</h3>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="cell">
        <span class="ic">Refunds</span>
        <h4>Handled by Apple</h4>
        <p>Purchases and refunds run entirely through the App Store. We never see a card
        number, and we cannot process a refund ourselves — Apple's request form is the
        route, and it is a generous one.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cell">
        <span class="ic">Devices</span>
        <h4>iPhone and iPad, one purchase</h4>
        <p>A universal app: buy it once and it installs on both, on every device signed
        into the same Apple Account.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cell">
        <span class="ic">Updates</span>
        <h4>Included, not a new SKU</h4>
        <p>Updates to v1 are free. If a future major version is ever charged for
        separately, it will be a separate app on the store, not a subscription bolted
        onto this one.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cell">
        <span class="ic">Trial</span>
        <h4>There isn't one</h4>
        <p>A paid-up-front app cannot offer a trial on the App Store. That is why the
        <a href="/docs">documentation</a> and the <a href="/compare">comparison</a> are
        blunt about the limits — you should be able to tell before paying.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cell">
        <span class="ic">Regions</span>
        <h4>Most of the world at launch</h4>
        <p>Available across the App Store's regions including the EU, with local pricing
        converted by Apple. Mainland China is not in the first release — it requires an
        MIIT filing we do not yet hold.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cell">
        <span class="ic">Business</span>
        <h4>Why it is this cheap</h4>
        <p>One developer, no servers to run, no support desk to staff. The costs that
        force other terminals onto a subscription are costs this app does not have.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Still deciding?</h3>
    <p class="txt">Read <a href="/compare">how Moshpit compares</a> — it names the cases
    where another terminal serves you better — or start with
    <a href="/docs/intro">what Moshpit does</a>. Neither page is a sales pitch.</p>
  </section>
</div>`;
---

<Marketing
  title="Pricing — Moshpit"
  description="Moshpit is $6.99, once. No subscription, no account, no in-app purchases. The launch price goes up, never down."
  path="pricing"
  lang="en"
>
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Marketing>
